if we look at the numbers of lives lost  in hospital you can see the rate at  which those numbers are increasing has  slowed today the number went up slightly  having said that 888 deaths were  recorded across the UK of course it is  unimaginable that we're talking about  these sorts of numbers and hundreds of  people losing their lives every day but  this does suggest that the curve is  continuing to flatten and it does fit in  with the longer-term trend that we're  seeing so if you look at the start of  this month the number of deaths were  doubling every three or so days now the  numbers of deaths are doubling every  eleven day so the rate has slowed but if  we continue at this slower rate we can  expect to pass twenty thousand deaths by  the end of next week and you'll remember  the government's chief scientific  adviser said it would be a good outcome  if we did it past twenty thousand deaths  it is looking likely that that will  happen now experts have long warned that  the key to tackling this virus is  testing and we have been looking at the  numbers of tests that are being done  across the country and you can see the  number of tests being done are rising  today twenty 1389 more tests have been  carried out but that is still only about  a fifth of that government target of a  hundred thousand tests a day by the end  of the month and if we look at where we  are compared to other countries you can  see we are below countries like South  Korea and Germany both of whom have been  seen to be very good at testing I've  seen relatively low numbers of deaths  but we're also below other European  countries like Italy and France it is  worth saying the way these figures are  collected is different so it does make  it difficult to make direct comparisons  but so far only not 0.5% of the  population here have been tested and if  testing is the key to getting through  this crisis this data does seem to  suggest that we are behind
 

